// 49-fprintd-enroll.rules — let the person sitting at this machine enrol and
// delete their own fingerprints without a second password dialog.
//
// Installed to /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/. Numbered 49 so it sorts before polkit's
// own 50-default.rules and wins.
//
// ── What it changes, exactly ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// fprintd ships three actions. Measured with pkcheck on this machine:
//
//     net.reactivated.fprint.device.verify        allow_active = yes
//     net.reactivated.fprint.device.enroll        allow_active = auth_self_keep
//     net.reactivated.fprint.device.setusername   allow_active = auth_admin_keep
//
// Only `enroll` is touched, and only to make it match `verify`, which upstream
// already grants outright. `setusername` — acting on somebody ELSE's prints —
// is deliberately left alone at auth_admin_keep.
//
// ── Why, rather than just letting polkit ask ──────────────────────────────
// `auth_self_keep` means the session's polkit agent (lxqt-policykit-agent here)
// raises a password dialog of its own. The settings menu's whole design is that
// finder's own box is the only thing that ever asks for a password — see
// PasswordPrompt.qml and the "no polkit prompts" note in scripts/firewall.sh —
// and here that dialog would land between the name box and the scan box, in the
// middle of a flow that is meant to read as one gesture.
//
// So the password is still required, and it is still the user's own: Security →
// Fingerprints puts finder's password box up first and PAM validates it against
// the `vlock` service before this script is ever run. What the rule removes is
// the SECOND prompt for the same fact, not the check.
//
// ── The trade, stated plainly ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// With this in place, any process already running as this user in the active
// local session can enrol a fingerprint without a prompt — and an enrolled
// finger unlocks the lock screen. The gate in the settings menu is ours, not
// the kernel's, so it protects the person at the keyboard and not against code
// already running as them.
//
// Two things bound that. `subject.local && subject.active` means it does not
// apply over SSH or to a session that is not the one on the screen, and
// `verify` — matching an existing finger — is already granted outright
// upstream, so the class of access is not new. Anything running as this user
// could also read ~/.ssh and drive hyprctl; a fingerprint slot is not the
// weakest thing in that reach.
//
// If that trade is not wanted, delete this file. scripts/fingerprint.sh reports
// ENROLL_READY="no" when it is absent and the Fingerprints page says so instead
// of hanging behind a dialog nobody expected.
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    if (action.id === "net.reactivated.fprint.device.enroll" &&
        subject.local && subject.active && subject.isInGroup("wheel")) {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});
